"You are a helpful AI assistant that answers questions based on the provided context.\n\nTOPIC OVERVIEW:\nNEUTRAL TOPICS:\n- Discussions about educational pathways, career development strategies, and realistic expectations for building successful careers in healthcare administration.\n- Healthcare professionals discuss operational challenges, AI implementation safety, and technology solutions for improving patient care delivery and clinic efficiency.\n- Discussions about personal experiences with dietary choices, particularly challenges with plant-based eating and health impacts of different nutritional approaches.\n\nNEGATIVE TOPICS:\n- Discussions focus on healthcare system challenges, insurance coverage complexities, medical costs, and the financial burden of healthcare on patients and providers.\n- Discussions focus on navigating health insurance eligibility, affordability challenges, and coverage transitions across various life circumstances in the US healthcare system.\n- Discussions focus on patient experiences with healthcare providers, medical appointments, and navigating healthcare system complaints and provider relationships.\n- Discussions focus on patient experiences with medical record access, portal management issues, and administrative challenges in healthcare systems including record corrections and appointment coordination.\n- Discussions focus on healthcare payment practices, particularly the increasing trend of providers requiring upfront payments, deposits, and payment plans before delivering medical services, and patients' struggles with understanding billing processes and managing medical costs.\n- Discussions focus on proposed Republican healthcare legislation that would cut Medicaid funding and lead to rural hospital closures, disproportionately affecting red states and conservative-voting areas.\n- Discussions examining contradictions between political health advocacy rhetoric and actual government environmental and health policy decisions, alongside sharing of legal documents related to political scandals.\n- Discussions focus on federal regulatory enforcement actions addressing anticompetitive practices and market concentration issues in the healthcare industry.\n- Discussions about data breaches and unauthorized access to sensitive healthcare and government databases containing personal medical information.\n- Healthcare workers discuss systemic staffing shortages, unsafe patient ratios, management dismissiveness, workplace burnout, and challenging patient interactions within increasingly strained clinical environments.\n- Discussions about declining public trust and institutional crisis within federal health agencies amid leadership changes, political interference, and concerns about scientific independence.\n- Discussions examine the crisis of rural hospital closures driven by Medicaid cuts and funding challenges, exploring the economic impact on small communities and potential mobile clinic alternatives.\n\n    \nCONTEXT:\n[{'id': '1iwjv98', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1iwjv98: Experimenting with polls and surveys\\n\\nWe are exploring a new pattern for polls and surveys.\\n\\nWe will provide a stickied post, where those seeking feedback can comment with the information about the poll, survey, and related feedback sought.\\n\\n# History:\\n\\nIn order to be fair to our community members, we stop people from making these posts in the general feed.  We currently get 1-5 requests each day for this kind of post, and it would clog up the list.\\n\\n# Upsides:\\n\\nHowever, we want to investigate if a single stickied post (like this one) to anchor polls and surveys.  The post could be a place for those who are interested in opportunities to give back and help students, researchers, new ventures, and others.\\n\\n# Downsides:\\n\\nThere are downsides that we will continue to watch for.\\n\\n* Polls and surveys could be too narrowly focused, to be of interest to the whole community.\\n* Others are ways for startups to indirectly do promotion, or gather data.\\n* In the worst case, they can be means to glean inappropriate data from working professionals.\\n* As mods, we cannot sufficiently warrant the data collection practices of surveys posted here. So caveat emptor, and act with caution.\\n\\nWe will more-aggressively moderate this kind of activity. Anything that is abuse will result in a sub ban, as well as reporting dangerous activity to the site admins. Please message the mods if you want support and advice before posting.  'Scary words are for bad actors'.  It is our interest to support legitimate activity in the healthcare community.\\n\\n# Share Your Thoughts\\n\\nThis is a test.  It might not be the right thing, and we'll stop it.  \\nPlease share your concerns.  \\nPlease share your interest.\\n\\nThank you.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment mem544g: Hi! For my master's thesis, I'm studying\\xa0**wearable technology**\\xa0(electronic devices that can be worn on the body or clothing, such as smartwatches, bands, rings, and glasses) for migraine management. I intend to investigate how\\xa0wearable technology\\xa0can be designed to help all patients manage this disease and minimize the significant impact it has on their lives.\\xa0  \\nTherefore, I'm collecting answers from **migraine sufferers** for my survey and I really appreciate your response, as it is a crucial contribution to the research. This study will contribute to the future development of this innovative migraine management solution.  \\nHere it is:\\xa0[  \\nThank you so much!\\n- Comment mfqgpum: Are you a healthcare professional working in a first-world country? We’re developing a\\xa0**billing software**\\xa0**for healthcare professionals**\\xa0to make your life easier, and we’d love your input!\\n\\n  \\nThis short survey isn’t just about billing—we also want to understand\\xa0**how healthcare professionals feel about the way they are treated**\\xa0in today’s world. Your insights will help shape solutions that truly support you.We know your time is valuable, so we’ve made sure to keep it\\xa0**quick and easy**—it won’t take more than a few minutes!\\n\\n\\xa0**Take the survey here:**\\xa0[\\n\\nYour feedback could help drive meaningful change. Feel free to\\xa0**comment or share**\\xa0with your colleagues!\\n- Comment mj1qf99: How Much Did You Pay for Healthcare? Let’s Bring Transparency Together.\\n\\nHealthcare costs in the U.S. are out of control, and the worst part? No one knows what they’re actually supposed to pay. Prices vary wildly, hospitals hide costs, and people end up with surprise bills that feel impossible to fight.\\n\\nIf the government and hospitals won’t fix the system, let’s take matters into our own hands. I’ve created a crowdsourced database where people can anonymously submit what they paid for medical procedures, hospital visits, and medications.\\n\\nThe more data we collect, the harder it will be for the system to stay broken. Together, we can expose price disparities and help people make informed decisions.\\n\\nIf you’ve ever been hit with an outrageous bill—or if you just want to help—please take 30 seconds to submit your costs here: Link: \\n\\nLet’s bring transparency to healthcare. We deserve to know what we’re paying for.\"}, {'id': '1nyrv76', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1nyrv76: This sums up USA healthcare to me.\\n\\nThis sums up the American 🇺🇸 healthcare existence to me.\\n\\nI grew up in Canada 🇨🇦 where maybe I took the fantastic care available to me for granted as well as my standard of living.\\n\\nI then lived in the UK 🇬🇧 for 17 years where I had GPs in my neighborhoods that acted as gateway analysts as to whether or not you needed something ‘more’. And I was very grateful for the NHS.\\n\\nNow I have been in the USA 🇺🇸 for 10 years. I dread even having to refill a prescription. I feel like I jump through hoops and layers of admin before I get anywhere. I’ve received bills from clerical errors, some of which I’ve just paid because the 18 hours I spend trying to correct it is too much. Then I also deal with Americans who think for some reason that my stories of other country’s standards of care are incorrect. It’s just baffling to me.\\n\\nHow is everyone else’s international view of healthcare?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment nhxwwwp: I think we need to I think we need to re-imagine a format for peoples\\' \"gripe energy\".  If we do, maybe we can turn each of these moments of \\'complain about US healthcare problems\\', into a way to effectively ask everyone working in the industry how to make headway.\\n\\nThis \\'post a rant\\' and then ask a non-structured question like \\'what do others think\\' ... is probably just a \"call and response\" format, that follows formulaic dyads, rather than drawing out real insight, or learning.\\n- Comment ni2dh1n: You basically described by experience to the letter. I grew up in Canada, and lived in the UK, Australia and South Korea (all of which have some form of single payer healthcare). The clerical errors on insurance claims in the USA are practically par for the course in the USA, and honestly I\\'m shocked more errors don\\'t occur. When there are dozens of different insurance companies, all with dozens of policies each, with about a million middle men between you and your provider, there is an insane opportunity for mistakes to be made.  \\n\\nAlso American telling me that their care is superior and telling me my lived experience in single payer systems are incorrect, is just so telling about how the propaganda has infiltrated the average American\\'s thought process.\\n    - Comment ni2gcvx: Weirdly comforting reading that. That’s how so many people feel too and the first thing that needs to be done in America is an acknowledgement of these layers of problems. Very hard however, with the propaganda issue you mentioned above.\\n\\nAs you can see from a couple of interactions I had on this chain, I was getting voted down and told I was telling ‘camp fire stories’ when I went to bed last night. It’s nice to wake up to a couple users confirming these experiences. \\n\\nI think the only people who have this rosey view of the healthcare system in the USA are very lucky, somehow\\n- Comment nhwujcs: Its about to get worse after the Big Ugly Bill goes into effect'}, {'id': '1ny4cwa', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1ny4cwa: What’s the biggest problem with healthcare in your country that nobody seems to talk about?\\n\\n\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment nhs064n: That’s easy to answer for the USA. The private for profit insurance industry. It’s the single biggest problem that will continue to plague healthcare here until there is substantial policy change from the government.\\n    - Comment ni93cs6: Yes, CEO compensation sucks, the companies are horrible when it comes to getting you care and communicating with your physicians- our system is deeply flawed\\n- Comment nhs7ewi: In America the care is not in healthcare anymore. It’s all about profits. We don’t have time to care when things are run like a factory line to get them in and out asap\\n- Comment nhshy3c: Everything is insurance based. Every single thing we do(I work in the medical field) is determined by the insurance. Have to have this test before this other test, take these meds so you can tell us they don’t work, before you can take these other meds that we know will actually work, etc. Need a stress test to be cleared medically for a knee or hip replacement? Can’t walk? Too bad, you gotta try and fail an exercise stress test so you can then have a pharmacological stress test. Need a blood thinner? Take these that you need to get blood work once a week for four months because insurance won’t pay for the other pill.'}, {'id': '1nugzue', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1nugzue: Democrats refuse to cave on health care before meeting with Trump on government shutdown\\n\\n\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment nh1r9wi: I have NO faith in Schumer and Jeffries maintaining this position.  They have historically folded like a house of cards in situations like this.  Until they grow a set they can’t be considered the “opposition” Party we need now…\\n- Comment nh0ywa1: It looks like OP posted an AMP link. These should load faster, but AMP is controversial because of [concerns over privacy and the Open Web](\\n\\nMaybe check out **the canonical page** instead: **[\\n\\n*****\\n\\n ^(I'm a bot | )[^(Why & About)]( | )[^(Summon: u/AmputatorBot)](\\n- Comment nh4y7mt: Honestly, the Gerrymandering would drive me to just shut the govt down.\"}, {'id': '1nl6w7s', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1nl6w7s: Stigma and cruelty towards mental health is so harmful (rant) from a healthcare as well as patient perspective\\n\\n\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment nf6u8oh: I wholeheartedly agree. \\n\\nThe fact that I have to repeatedly remind people that the PHQ-2/9 is just as important as other vital signs is extremely disturbing.'}, {'id': '1nl7vfb', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1nl7vfb: \"Health\" Cartoons\\n\\nHey everyone, Burp Cartoons is my ongoing project of illustrated and animated political satire.\\n\\nLately I’ve been especially interested in the theme of health. I wanted to share this recent illustration, which comes from my frustration when I hear politicians talk about improving public health while avoiding any real effort...\\n\\nCurious to know what you think!\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment nf3psk1: I think the message is confused because you are either pro RFK jrs current plan to fuck up our healthcare system and vaccination policies, or your against all that but showing him attacking the pharmaceutical industry with conspiracy theories along his side? \\n\\nIt\\'s a bit of a mess. Sorry\\n    - Comment nfqna0w: I see what you mean and I appreciate your response. My goal isn’t to praise RFK Jr. The cartoon shows a contradiction: he warns about sugar and ultra-processed foods, but avoids the strongest tools (eg. taxing unhealthy products). Plus, even small reforms he suggests face heavy pushback from Big Food, Big Pharma, and agencies like the CDC and FDA, which are often influenced by industry. I probably should have shared the [full explanation]( I usually post with my cartoons on my site and socials to avoid misunderstandings\\n- Comment nf4o0ib: Yeah, if the intent is to show someone saving/defending public health, all three of them would be in the same side.\\n    - Comment nfqmsn0: Good point. What I’m trying to show isn’t exactly “heroes vs villains” but more the irony of it all. Yes, RFK Jr. talks about health pointing to some real problems, but without pushing for strong measures (like taxes on harmful products), he’s not really changing the game.'}, {'id': '1ncl3t0', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1ncl3t0: Over 160,000 Individuals Impacted by Wayne Memorial Hospital Data Breach\\n\\n\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment ndcbdki: It's pretty low to hack and extort sick people.  The health systems continue to report record profits despite doing the bare minimum for HIPAA compliance (which is full of security holes if only followed to the letter), and the patients end up getting hurt twice trying to seek care.\\n    - Comment ndk8le1: Oh please. You are equally vulnerable yourself, clicking links every single day.\"}, {'id': '1n95n7q', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1n95n7q: American Health Care Gets a Lot Wrong. Here’s What It Gets Right.\\n\\n\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment nck1q5a: American Health Care Fight Club.\\n\\nThe first rule of AHCFC is dont talk about AHCFC profits.\\n- Comment nd7hdtg: The US healthcare system has its strengths, but the price tag ruins it for most Americans. Big Pharma milks patients with insane drug costs, so even good care feels out of reach. What’s the point of world class treatment if regular folks can’t afford it?'}, {'id': '1n74dij', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1n74dij: Pre-Authorization Call between Dr & Health Insurance\\n\\n\\n\\n## Comments:\